# Drafted by Gemini (R11c connections_custom), edited.

~ morgan_start
morgan [weary]: Hold a moment, friend. You have got that same blank look in your eyes that I see in the water bucket every morning.
Narrator: A scrap offolded paper rustles between {name}'s fingers, smelling faintly of damp wool and pressed lavender.
morgan [neutral]: Like you woke up in the heather with your pockets empty and your own name feeling like a borrowed coat.
* Hum the three notes that keep returning to you -> morgan_song
* Ask how long they have lived without a past -> morgan_blank
* Say your past is none of their concern -> morgan_quiet

~ morgan_song
Player [weary]: It goes low, then catches on the third note. Like a cradle song with the middle torn out.
morgan [smile]: Wait. Da-dum, then the drop into the minor? Saints above, I have whistled that second half while sweeping the hearth for three winters.
Narrator: For the space of a breath, the two halves fit together like a cracked plate mended with warm wax.
-> morgan_pamphlet

~ morgan_blank
morgan [sad]: Two years come the next tolling, give or take. Folks here are kind enough, provided you pull your weight and do not ask where the road leads.
Player [neutral]: And you never found a single thread to pull?
-> morgan_pamphlet

~ morgan_quiet
morgan [neutral]: Fair enough, I will not pry. When you have nothing in your head but mist, even the mist feels like private property.
-> morgan_pamphlet

~ morgan_pamphlet
morgan [sly]: Take a look at this handbill I found tucked in a crate of salt. It speaks of a house called Memory's Rest, out where the forgetful go to sit.
-> morgan_close

~ morgan_close
morgan [smile]: Keep your ears open for the rest of that tune, friend. If you find out who we were, save a verse for me.
set connection_apocalypse
attitude morgan friendly
-> END

~ baltus_start
baltus_tolliver [smile]: Pardon the intrusion, traveler, but my nose rarely deceives me. That is coal smoke, cold iron, and river fog clinging to your hem.
Narrator: He tips his hat with a gloved hand, and the faint chime of a brass ticket punch clicks inside his waistcoat pocket.
baltus_tolliver [sly]: Only one engine in all of Druskenvald burns fuel like that. You have ridden the Ghostlight Express before tonight, have you not?
* Admit you remember the whistle and the green lamps -> baltus_train
* Ask what a village merchant knows of that train -> baltus_inquiry
* Step back and tell him he is mistaken -> baltus_deny

~ baltus_train
Player [neutral]: I know the sound of those wheels on the trestle. Once you hear it, ordinary iron sounds dead.
baltus_tolliver [smile]: Quite so, quite so. A passenger of distinction never truly steps off the platform, no matter how far inland they wander.
-> baltus_river

~ baltus_inquiry
baltus_tolliver [neutral]: A man of commerce must watch the timetables, my dear friend. Freight moves by rail, by cart, and by darker conveyances when the moon allows.
-> baltus_river

~ baltus_deny
baltus_tolliver [smile]: Of course, my mistake entirely. The moorland damp plays curious tricks on velvet and wool alike.
-> baltus_river

~ baltus_river
baltus_tolliver [sly]: Still, should you ever long for that particular furnace heat again, look downriver. The Stranger keeps another engine churning inside a paddlewheel hull.
Narrator: The lantern light catches the rim of his spectacles, turning his eyes into two flat coins of yellow glass.
-> baltus_close

~ baltus_close
baltus_tolliver [neutral]: Do call on my counter whenever your purse feels heavy. We understand one another, you and I, and I always favor repeat travelers.
set connection_deliverance
attitude baltus_tolliver friendly
-> END

~ finneas_start
finneas_trout [afraid]: Do not move your boots. Stand right where the lantern hits the gravel, if you please.
Narrator: The schoolmaster squints past {name}'s shoulder at the flagstones, where {name}'s shadow stretches a thumb's breadth too late to match the step.
finneas_trout [weary]: Merciful heaven, there really are two of you. Or rather, there is you, and there is the piece of you that slipped its leash.
* Ask where he has seen your likeness before -> finneas_double
* Ask what he means by a piece slipping loose -> finneas_grey
* Tell him to mind his primers and chalk -> finneas_warn

~ finneas_double
finneas_trout [afraid]: Three nights before the butcherings down by the sheepfold, someone with your exact stride passed my schoolhouse window. No lantern, no footfall on thefrost, heading north-east.
Player [neutral]: If it wore my face, it did not ask my leave to borrow it.
-> finneas_grey

~ finneas_warn
finneas_trout [neutral]: Scold me if you must, yet a man who teaches children to count knows when one and one make a wrong number.
-> finneas_grey

~ finneas_grey
finneas_trout [sly]: I have kept notes on what the old monographs call the Grey. When a soul enters Druskenvald crookedly, the reflection and the meat divide at the threshold.
Narrator: Chalk dust puffs from his cuff as he traces a split circle in the air between you.
-> finneas_close

~ finneas_close
finneas_trout [weary]: Watch the puddles and the dark windowpanes, traveler. Whatever walked north-east toward the hills is already practicing your handwriting.
set connection_duality
attitude finneas_trout friendly
-> END

~ jean_start
jean_myriel [neutral]: Turn your wrist over, stranger. No, the left one, where the cuff is frayed.
Narrator: Heat rolls off the forge behind him, carrying the sharp, coppery tang of quenched steel and old winter sweat.
jean_myriel [weary]: I thought so. Only a Crimson aspirant holds a blade with the thumb high like a lancet, waiting for the vein to rise.
* Show him the old scar along your palm -> jean_blood
* Ask where a village smith learned the Rose's grip -> jean_past
* Pull your hand back and say nothing -> jean_silent

~ jean_blood
Player [neutral]: The ink faded when I crossed the mist, but the calluses stayed. We were taught that iron is thirsty until it drinks.
jean_myriel [sad]: Aye, and the thirst never leaves the metal once you wake it. I folded my share of red steel before I laid the tongs down for good.
-> jean_monastery

~ jean_past
jean_myriel [weary]: I wore the briar cord at my waist twenty years back. Tempered three swords in warm basins before I realized what the steel was taking from the smith.
-> jean_monastery

~ jean_silent
jean_myriel [neutral]: Keep your secrets if they warm you. An old anvil knows the hammer's weight whether the smith speaks or not.
-> jean_monastery

~ jean_monastery
jean_myriel [angry]: Hear me plain: if you go looking for the brothers at Dawn's Gate, turn your heels back to the valley. That monastery is a slaughterhouse dressed in stained glass.
-> jean_close

~ jean_close
jean_myriel [neutral]: Here in Wickermoor, I mend plowshares and horse shoes, and nobody bleeds for a hinge. Come by the forge if you want honest iron.
set connection_immortality
attitude jean_myriel friendly
-> END

~ solomon_start
solomon_roderick [angry]: Keep your hands where the torchlight can find them, stranger. You walk with your shoulders crooked, like someone used to bowing under a low lintel.
Narrator: Stone dust coats the mason's knuckles, and his chisel taps a dry, steady warning against his leather apron.
solomon_roderick [weary]: I know that stoop. Every night since the frost set in, a crooked man stands at the foot of my bed and bends his neck the exact same way.
* Tell him you left the circle and broke your oath -> solomon_dream
* Ask what else he sees in those nightmares -> solomon_coven
* Say a stiff neck is no crime in a damp country -> solomon_stone

~ solomon_dream
Player [weary]: I knelt in the ashes once, mason, before I knew what was waiting behind the branches. I am done kneeling.
solomon_roderick [neutral]: Are you now? That is easy to say in the square with lanterns burning, and harder when the thatch starts whispering at three in the morning.
-> solomon_coven

~ solomon_stone
solomon_roderick [sly]: Damp gets in the knees, traveler, not the soul. You know the cadence of the old litany, do not lie to a man who cuts headstones.
-> solomon_coven

~ solomon_coven
solomon_roderick [afraid]: In the dream, the Crooked Man counts his servants on long, knotted fingers. Three names belong to the coven out in the timber, and their sign is carved under my eaves.
Narrator: He rubs his thumb across a scarred groove on his forearm, his breathing slow and gravelly.
-> solomon_close

~ solomon_close
solomon_roderick [neutral]: Maybe a runaway hound is best for hunting the pack. Come talk to me by the yard when the square clears, and we will trade names.
set connection_kindred
attitude solomon_roderick friendly
-> END

~ eleanor_start
eleanor_heyling [sly]: Do not rub at your eyes like that, dear, you will only make the bruising darker. Printer's gall and graveyard tallow leave a very specific stain on the lids.
Narrator: The bookseller smells of dry vellum, cloves, and mouse-chewed linen as she peers over her half-moon spectacles.
eleanor_heyling [smile]: You have the sickness of the uncut folio. Someone has been reading chapters that were meant to stay sewn shut.
* Recite the third footnote from the Verdigris Concord -> eleanor_cite
* Ask if she carries anything worth the headache -> eleanor_places
* Claim it is only lack of sleep from the road -> eleanor_sickness

~ eleanor_cite
Player [sly]: "Wherein the seventh bell is cast without a tongue, lest the parish hear the metal confess." Second edition, bound in salted eel-skin.
eleanor_heyling [smile]: Oh, delightful! Though the third edition corrects "parish" to "abbey" just before the censor's fire took the press.
-> eleanor_places

~ eleanor_sickness
eleanor_heyling [neutral]: Road dust washes off with cold water, my friend. A crooked footnote stays behind the brow-bone and ticks like a beetle in the wainscoting.
-> eleanor_places

~ eleanor_places
eleanor_heyling [sly]: My shelves here are mostly almanacs and harmless pastorals, for my health's sake. What you crave is split three ways: an island, a sunken library, and a dead bailiff's ledger.
Narrator: Her fingernail traces the gilt spine of a ledger on her counter, leaving a thin line in the dust.
-> eleanor_close

~ eleanor_close
eleanor_heyling [smile]: Come back when you have a quiet hour and a dry candle. It is a rare treat to meet another reader who knows books can bite.
set connection_malediction
attitude eleanor_heyling friendly
-> END

~ weston_start
weston_murdoch [smile]: Well now, bless the road and the mud upon it! Look at the weave of that cloak, neighbors, that is provincial wool if ever I saw a loom.
Narrator: The preacher's voice rings out warm as spiced cider across the cold cobbles, his hands open and ungloved.
weston_murdoch [neutral]: You are no stranger to the crooked moon, friend, only new to our little hollow. Which province claimed your prayers before you walked into Wickermoor?
* Sing him a harvesting round from the outer parishes -> weston_song
* Tell him your home kept candles for every altar -> weston_faiths
* Say one dark sky looks much like another -> weston_province

~ weston_song
Player [smile]: "A sheaf for the rain, a stone for the gate, and a song for the bird that wakes too late." We sang it whenever the wagons came in whole.
weston_murdoch [smile]: Ha! Hear that cadence! Three faiths braided into four bars of music, and not a sour note among them.
-> weston_close

~ weston_faiths
weston_murdoch [smile]: That is the grand old way of the provinces. An oak bough over the barn, a sunflower on the table, and a songbird carved into the cradle.
Player [neutral]: Folks here in the village seem to prefer choosing a single fence and standing behind it.
-> weston_close

~ weston_province
weston_murdoch [neutral]: Ah, the road makes cynics of us all when the boots wear thin. Still, your tongue carries the rhythm of the old parish bells.
-> weston_close

~ weston_close
weston_murdoch [smile]: Our choir can always use a voice that knows Druskenvald is wider than one village square. Walk in grace, cousin, and my door is ever unlatched.
set connection_rapture
attitude weston_murdoch friendly
-> END
